{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"01","chapter_title":"Course Completion and Transfer","title":"Capstone Artifact Review","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on capstone artifact review, centered on a review checklist, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the capstone artifact review checklist shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer","cat100_supplement_capstone_artifact_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/07/08"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_calibration"]},"content":"# Capstone Artifact Review\n\n![Capstone Artifact Review textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-01-capstone-artifact-review.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe capstone artifact review is the moment when everything you built across CAT 100 stops being homework and starts being a public record of what you can do. A hiring manager, a transfer advisor, or a scholarship reviewer will not read your weekly assignments; they will open one spreadsheet, one website, or one resume and decide in about thirty seconds whether to trust it. That decision is shaped by something you usually cannot see while you are building: whether your files quietly leak private information.\n\nA privacy checklist is the discipline of inspecting your own artifacts the way an attacker or a careless search engine would. The most common failure in a student portfolio is not an ugly layout; it is an API key, a password, an email address, or a classmate's name committed into a public GitHub repository where it stays forever. This section teaches you to run a concrete, repeatable scan before review, so the artifact you publish is both useful and safe to share.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the capstone artifact review checklist shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nMaya is about to share her GitHub Pages portfolio in the capstone showcase. The site looks finished: a clean header, three project cards, a contact form. What she cannot see from the rendered page is that her `app.js` still holds a weather API key she pasted while testing, and her `contact.html` carries a leftover comment with an SMTP password.\n\nThe rendered website hides both. A reviewer browsing the live page would never notice. But in a public GitHub repository, automated bots scrape new commits for exactly these strings within minutes. Maya's artifact is visually polished and simultaneously a security incident. The privacy checklist exists to catch the gap between \"looks done\" and \"is safe to publish.\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA privacy checklist is a fixed set of inspections you run against the actual files you are about to publish, not against your memory of how careful you were. It rests on one hard fact about version control and the open web: once something is committed and pushed to a public repository, it is permanent. Deleting a secret in a later commit does not remove it; the value still lives in the repository history and may already be cached by scrapers. The only safe response to a leaked credential is to rotate it (generate a new one and invalidate the old).\n\nThe checklist covers three distinct surfaces, and confusing them is where students get hurt:\n\n| Surface | What to look for | Why it matters |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Accounts and credentials | API keys, passwords, tokens, `.env` contents in tracked files | A live key can be abused to run up charges or send mail in your name |\n| Data sharing | Real names, emails, student IDs, anything tied to a person who did not consent | FERPA-style privacy and basic respect; classmates did not agree to be published |\n| AI tool boundaries | What you pasted into an AI tool, and what it generated that you cannot verify | Pasting private data into a chatbot shares it with a third party |\n\nThe reason a checklist beats good intentions is that secrets enter files by accident, during testing, when you are focused on making something work. You will not remember them later. A scan finds them; a memory does not. The output that earns review is not \"I was careful,\" it is a reproducible command and its result.\n\n> **Recent evidence (as of 2026).** Since March 11, 2024, GitHub enables secret scanning and push protection by default on new public repositories owned by personal accounts, so an obvious API-key push is often blocked before it ever lands; push protection blocked roughly 4.4 million secrets in 2024. But the guardrail recognizes only a limited set of known token types (200+ patterns from 180+ providers), can be bypassed or overridden, and does not scan a repository's existing history. The manual checklist, plus a dedicated scanner such as gitleaks (fast pre-commit/diff regex scanning) or trufflehog (full-history scanning that also verifies whether a found credential is still live), still matters.\n\n<inline-check question=\"You deleted an API key from app.js in a new commit and pushed it. Is the leak fixed?\" explanation=\"Deletion does not remove the value from a public repository’s history; a clone still exposes the old commit. The only safe response to any pushed credential is to rotate it.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, the current file no longer contains the key, so it is safe.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, the key still lives in the commit history; you must rotate it.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, as long as the commit message does not mention the key.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Auditing One Repo Against the Privacy Checklist\n\nA student is about to submit a personal portfolio site hosted on GitHub Pages. Before the capstone review, they run the three-line privacy checklist against the repo.\n\n**Step 1. Scan for committed secrets.** Open the project and search the tracked files.\n\n```\n$ git grep -n -i \"key\\|password\\|token\"\napp.js:14:  const WEATHER_API_KEY = \"a1f4c9e7b2d6\";\ncontact.html:32:  <!-- TODO: SMTP password = summer2026! -->\n```\n\nTwo hits. Both are real secrets sitting in tracked files.\n\n**Step 2. Decide and act, row by row.**\n\n| Item | Finding | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `app.js:14` weather API key | Live key in a public repo | Move to `/config/keys.js`, add that path to `.gitignore`, rotate the key |\n| `contact.html:32` SMTP password | Password in an HTML comment | Delete the comment, store the password in the server env, rotate it |\n| Old commits | Key already pushed in commit `a1b2c3` | History is public, so treat both secrets as leaked and rotate regardless of deletion |\n\n**Step 3. Record verifiable evidence.** The checklist line that passes review is not \"checked privacy.\" It is:\n\n> Removed weather key from `app.js:14` into `/config/keys.js` (now in `.gitignore`), deleted SMTP comment in `contact.html:32`, rotated both secrets on 2026-05-12, reran `git grep -i key` with zero hits in the working tree, and ran a history scan confirming no live credential remains in any commit; confirmed the page still loads.\n\nThat paragraph names the file, the line, the action, the date, and the re-check, so a reviewer can reproduce every step. Polish (a nicer layout, a hero image) is the last layer; it never substitutes for the secret audit.\n\n> **The scan has a blind spot.** `git grep` searches only the current working tree. Delete a pushed secret, re-run the scan, and you will get zero hits while the secret still lives in commit `a1b2c3`, exactly the false reassurance this section warns against. To search history, run `git grep <pattern> $(git rev-list --all)`, or use a dedicated scanner: gitleaks (fast regex scanning of diffs and pre-commit hooks) or trufflehog (scans the full history and verifies whether a found credential is still live). A clean result means zero hits in the working tree *and* a history scan confirming no live credential remains; either way, rotation, not deletion, is still the real fix.\n\nWork the scan as a fixed procedure. Reveal each step only after you have predicted what it does:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the privacy checklist on one repo\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You are about to publish a GitHub Pages portfolio. What is the first move before you trust the page looks clean?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Add a hero image so the site looks finished\",\"Scan the tracked files for secrets with git grep\",\"Email the link to a friend to check it loads\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Scan first. Run git grep -n -i for key, password, and token against the actual tracked files, not your memory of how careful you were.\"},{\"reveal\":\"For each hit, decide and act row by row: move the secret out of tracked files, add that path to .gitignore, and note the exact file and line.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Rotate any secret that was ever pushed. Deletion alone leaves the value in public history, so invalidate the old credential and issue a new one.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Record verifiable evidence: file, line, action, rotation date, a clean working-tree re-scan, and a history scan (git grep across git rev-list --all, or gitleaks/trufflehog) confirming no live credential remains. That paragraph is what earns review.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nThen practice the order itself. Drag these into the sequence a safe audit follows:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Scan tracked files with git grep for key, password, token\",\"Decide accept or remove for each flagged hit\",\"Move secrets out of tracked files and into .gitignore\",\"Rotate every credential that was ever pushed\",\"Re-scan and confirm zero hits, then log file, line, action, and date\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"cat100_supplement_capstone_artifact_review\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTreat any string that grants access or identifies a person as a leak until you have proven otherwise with a scan. Concretely, before you publish:\n\n1. **Scan, do not recall.** Run `git grep -i` for `key`, `password`, `token`, `secret`, and `@` (to catch emails). Trust the output, not your memory. Remember that `git grep` checks only the working tree; pair it with a history scan (`git grep <pattern> $(git rev-list --all)`, or gitleaks/trufflehog) before you call the repo clean.\n2. **Rotate anything that was ever pushed.** If a secret reached a public commit, deletion is not enough; invalidate the old credential and issue a new one.\n3. **Quarantine third-party data.** Anything you pasted into an AI tool has left your machine; if it included private data, treat that data as shared and disclose it.\n\nIf a string survives all three checks, it is safe to publish. If you skip the scan, \"it looks fine\" is a guess, not a decision.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is believing that deleting a secret fixes the leak. It does not: the value remains in the commit history of a public repository, retrievable by anyone who clones it. Deletion plus rotation is the fix; deletion alone is theater.\n\nA second misreading is treating private data as only passwords. A classmate's full name on a sample invoice, a real email in a contact form's default value, or a teammate's student ID in a test dataset are all data you are publishing without consent. The checklist protects people, not just credentials.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a real repository through the checklist in order. Begin with the credentials surface: open every file you wrote, not just the ones you remember editing, and run the grep scan. Treat each hit as guilty until cleared. The weather key in the worked example looks harmless because it is short and \"just for testing,\" but a live key in a public repo is exactly what bots harvest.\n\nMove next to the data surface. Look at sample data, form defaults, comments, and commit messages. A budget spreadsheet you built from a real club roster may carry member names; replace them with placeholders like `Member A` before publishing. This is where the artifact stops being only about you and starts being about the people whose information you handled.\n\nFinally, audit the AI boundary. Reconstruct what you actually pasted into any AI tool while building. If you pasted a code block that contained the API key to ask for help debugging, that key is now known outside your machine and must be rotated, exactly as if you had published it. The walkthrough ends with the evidence paragraph: file, line, action, date, and re-check.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a running list with two columns: the exact string you found and the exact remediation you performed. A strong reader of this section does not summarize \"I checked for secrets\"; they can point to `app.js:14`, the action taken, the rotation date, and the clean re-scan.\n\nA strong reader of the capstone privacy checklist can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which exact files and lines did the scan flag?\n2. For each flagged item, was the credential rotated, not just deleted?\n3. What real-person data appears in sample data or form defaults, and how was it removed?\n4. What did you paste into an AI tool, and does any of it now need rotation or disclosure?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can rerun your scan and get zero hits, and when every remediation is dated and verifiable. The standard is not \"I believe it is clean,\" it is a reproducible `git grep` command that returns nothing plus a short log of what was removed and rotated.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"Scanned the repo for credentials and personal data on 2026-05-12; moved one API key out of tracked files and rotated it, removed one password comment and rotated the mail credential, replaced two real member names in the sample budget with placeholders. Re-scan returns zero hits.\" That sentence proves the decision instead of asserting it.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same artifact into a stricter setting: a public job application where the repository link goes to a recruiter, or a team project where a partner committed the secrets. The scan does not change, but the stakes and the responsibility do. With a recruiter, a single leaked key signals carelessness about exactly the kind of access an employer would trust you with. With a partner, you must coordinate rotation because the leaked credential may be theirs. Use the change in setting to test whether you treat the checklist as a habit you can run anywhere, or only a one-time CAT 100 task.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the capstone artifact review checklist packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-01-capstone-artifact-review-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"capstone artifact review checklist\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the capstone artifact review checklist.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the capstone artifact review checklist for CAT 100 08.01. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_capstone_artifact_review\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_capstone_artifact_review","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_capstone_artifact_review","cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"],"stem":"Your privacy checklist row reads: 'API key for the weather widget is stored in app.js, line 14, and pushed to the public GitHub Pages repo.' What does the checklist tell you to fix before the capstone review?","options":["Nothing; a weather key is low risk and the page already works.","Move the key out of app.js into a config the repo ignores, rotate the exposed key, and note both actions in the checklist.","Rename app.js to app.min.js so the key is harder to read.","Add a comment in app.js asking reviewers not to copy the key."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A key committed to a public repo is already exposed in history. The defensible fix is to remove it from tracked files, rotate it, and document the change so a reviewer can verify it.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_privacy_public_repo_safe","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You committed secrets.txt with a database password in commit a1b2c3, then deleted the file in commit d4e5f6. Which statement is accurate?","options":["The password is gone because the latest commit no longer contains the file.","The password is still readable in commit a1b2c3, so you must rotate it and treat it as leaked.","Deleting the file in d4e5f6 overwrites the earlier commit automatically.","GitHub removes deleted files from history after 24 hours."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Git history is additive. Anyone can check out a1b2c3 and read secrets.txt, so the only safe response is to rotate the password.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_delete_equals_gone","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You want an AI tool to help format a CSV of 40 classmates' names, emails, and student IDs into a clean table. What is the correct privacy move before prompting?","options":["Paste the whole CSV so the AI sees the real structure.","Replace the real columns with fake values like 'Student A, a@example.com, 0001' and send only two sample rows plus the column headers.","Send the file but tell the AI to keep it confidential.","Email the AI provider asking permission to share the roster."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI needs the shape, not the people. Anonymized headers and two fake rows get the same formatting help without exposing real names, emails, or IDs.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_ai_paste_full_data","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_capstone_artifact_review_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which checklist entry is detailed enough for a reviewer to verify the privacy decision without asking you?","options":["'Checked privacy: looks fine.'","'Removed API key from app.js (line 14), added /config/keys.js to .gitignore, rotated the key on 2026-05-12, confirmed the page still loads.'","'I am pretty sure there are no passwords in the repo.'","'The site is done and the link works.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verifiable entry names the file, the line, the action, the date, and the confirmation, so a reviewer can re-check it directly.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_1_privacy_public_repo_safe","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"c1f7f46f8e9a9ed52e7f08cc5e3f4ed3d264c7a4b425b67e27d6c9be264df45a","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}